import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import DelegationExample from "../../components/DelegationExample.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Delegate codebase research — Triss"
  description="Ask focused questions about selected files, inspect source references, and use verified findings in your development workflow."
>
  <Header current="workflows" />

  <main class="workflow-layout">
    <div class="workflow-page-body">
      <p class="workflow-eyebrow">Research workflow</p>
      <h1 class="workflow-page-title">
        Understand unfamiliar code without handing over the whole repository.
      </h1>
      <p class="workflow-lede">
        Instead of pasting files into your main agent's context, delegate a focused
        question: <code>triss ask</code> reads only the sources you name, sends them to
        your selected provider, and returns findings with source references you can
        verify yourself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="workflow-page-body">
      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Use it when</h2>
        <p>
          You need an answer to a specific question about selected sources before you
          start changing them — how a mechanism works, where a decision is made, what a
          module enforces. The question is bounded, and so is the corpus: you choose
          exactly which files Triss reads.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Provide</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A precise question. Ask for the specific behavior, precedence, or constraint
            you need, and ask for cited lines.
          </li>
          <li>
            The selected files or globs. Quote globs so your shell passes them through
            to Triss instead of expanding them:
          </li>
        </ul>
        <pre tabindex="0" class="code-block"><code>triss ask --paths "src/**/*.js" --question "Where is request validation handled, and what does it enforce? Cite the relevant lines."</code></pre>
        <p>
          Do not include secret files such as <code>.env</code> in the corpus — what you
          select is what gets sent to your model provider. Directories are not an
          automatic recursive corpus: pass files or a glob such as
          <code>src/**/*.js</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Run</h2>
        <p>
          The command below is the recorded run shown later on this page. It was
          executed with <code>triss ask --provider zai</code> against a source file of
          the Triss repository itself, at a public commit.
        </p>
        <pre tabindex="0" class="code-block"><code>triss ask --provider zai --paths src/model-selection.js --question "How are provider, model, and execution engine selected? Cite the relevant lines and distinguish explicit request values from configured defaults."</code></pre>
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            This is an example recorded on Triss' own sources. In your own repository
            you choose your own paths and your own question — no project needs to
            contain <code>src/model-selection.js</code>. Use your configured provider
            by omitting <code>--provider</code>, or pass the provider you selected.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Inspect</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Open the cited files and lines and compare them with each claim.</li>
          <li>
            Identify what remains unknown: a focused answer about selected files is not
            a map of the whole repository.
          </li>
          <li>
            Keep the details your main agent needs — verified behavior, exact
            identifiers, line references — and drop the rest.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <DelegationExample id="recorded-example" />
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Bring it back</h2>
        <p>
          When the findings have been verified, hand them to your main agent as context
          for the change you are about to make:
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="workflow-example-label">Example instruction</p>
          <p>
            "Use these verified findings to plan the change. Re-open the relevant
            source before editing."
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          This is an example instruction you write yourself — not command output, and
          not a recorded result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="workflow-block">
        <h2 class="workflow-block__title">Limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The answer analyzes the context you passed. It is not proof that the whole
            repository is understood.
          </li>
          <li>The model can be wrong, even when it cites real lines.</li>
          <li>
            A follow-up query may be needed: narrower files, or a more specific
            question.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <nav class="workflow-next" aria-label="Next steps">
      <span class="workflow-next__label">Next:</span>
      <a href="/workflows/review/">Review workflow</a>
      <a href="/commands/">Commands</a>
      <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Quickstart</a>
    </nav>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .workflow-eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-label);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  .workflow-page-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.6vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text-strong);
  }
  .workflow-lede {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .workflow-example-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .workflow-next__label {
    color: var(--color-text-faint);
  }
</style>
